{
  "image_name": "2c3f325a563daacc215aa40fec44d75b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how Gram-negative bacteria build two major classes of outer-membrane proteins and highlights the distinct pathways for secretins and porins such as OmpF. At the top, a simplified cartoon contrasts these outcomes: porins are translated in the cytoplasm, moved across the inner membrane, and ultimately assembled into β-barrel arrays in the outer membrane, whereas secretins are translated and translocated but then form larger secretion-system structures.\n\nThe lower schematic places these pathways in the cell envelope. Proteins are first exported through the inner-membrane SecYEG translocon into the periplasm. From there, the porin pathway appears to feed into the BAM complex in the outer membrane, which inserts and folds β-barrel proteins. Secretins, in contrast, are shown following a different route involving a long periplasmic assembly path and factors associated with lipopolysaccharide handling, labeled with an LPS translocase. The outer membrane is depicted with lipopolysaccharides on its surface and peptidoglycan in the periplasm below it, emphasizing the envelope architecture through which these proteins must be delivered.\n\nOverall, the figure’s main message is that secretins and porins share early secretion steps but diverge in outer-membrane assembly and final organization.",
